1. Create a basic ServiceAccountCreate a file named serviceaccount.yaml.

apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: app-sa
  namespace: default

If you omit the namespace, Kubernetes normally uses the namespace from your current context, which defaults to default if none is configured.

2. Use the ServiceAccount in a Pod
Creating a ServiceAccount doesn't automatically assign it to your application. You can specify it in a Pod manifest.
If not specified it will take default ServiceAccount

apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod
metadata:
  name: app-pod
  namespace: default
spec:
  serviceAccountName: app-sa
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:1.27

spec:
  serviceAccountName: app-sa
It tells Kubernetes to run the Pod using the identity app-sa.


3. Control automatic token mounting

Kubernetes can provide a ServiceAccount token to a Pod so applications can authenticate to the API server. Modern Kubernetes commonly uses short-lived, projected tokens.

You can't run a Pod with "no ServiceAccount" at all. Every Pod always has one assigned. What automountServiceAccountToken: false does is stop the token from being mounted into the Pod. The Pod still has an identity assigned, but the credentials aren't inside the container, so the app can't authenticate to the API server.

You can disable automatic mounting when the application doesn't need Kubernetes API access.

apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: app-sa
  namespace: default
automountServiceAccountToken: false

you can also configure this on pod
spec:
  automountServiceAccountToken: false

On the ServiceAccount: applies to every Pod using that SA (unless a Pod overrides it).
On the Pod spec: applies to just that Pod, and takes precedence over the ServiceAccount setting.

default SA has no permissions beyond basic discovery by default (with RBAC enabled), but disabling the token removes the unnecessary exposure entirely.

4. Give the ServiceAccount permissions using RBAC

Let's use a real workplace scenario.

Your application needs to view Pods in the default namespace, but it must not create or delete them.

You need three YAML resources:

1. ServiceAccount
Identifies the application as app-sa.

2. Role
Allows reading Pods in one namespace.

3. RoleBinding
Connects the Role's permissions to app-sa.


1. Create the ServiceAccount
apiVersion: v1
kind: ServiceAccount
metadata:
  name: app-team1-sa
  namespace: default

2. Create a Role

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: Role
metadata:
  name: pod-reader
  namespace: default
rules:
  - apiGroups: [""]
    resources: ["pods"]
    verbs: ["get", "list", "watch"]

3. Bind the Role to the ServiceAccount

apiVersion: rbac.authorization.k8s.io/v1
kind: RoleBinding
metadata:
  name: app-pod-reader
  namespace: default
subjects:
  - kind: ServiceAccount
    name: app-sa
    namespace: default
roleRef:
  apiGroup: rbac.authorization.k8s.io
  kind: Role
  name: pod-reader

- subjects: Who receives the permissions.
- kind: ServiceAccount: The subject is an application identity.
- roleRef: Which Role contains the permissions.
- roleRef.kind: Role: The permissions are namespace-scoped.

root@quixydevops:/home/devopsprod# kubectl auth can-i list pods \
  --as=system:serviceaccount:sriya:sa \
  -n sriya
yes
root@quixydevops:/home/devopsprod# kubectl auth can-i delete pods   --as=system:serviceaccount:sriya:sa   -n sriya
no


